2b:T19bb,<p>Many vegetarians in India fall short on protein without realising it — not because vegetarian food lacks protein, but because our everyday meals lean heavily on grains. A <strong>high protein vegetarian salad</strong> is one of the easiest ways to close the gap at lunch.</p>
<h2 id="how-much-protein-do-you-actually-need">How much protein do you actually need?</h2><p>The Indian Council of Medical Research – National Institute of Nutrition (ICMR-NIN) puts the requirement for healthy adults at about 0.83 g of protein per kilogram of body weight per day (<a href="https://www.nin.res.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ICMR-NIN, Nutrient Requirements for Indians, 2020</a>). For someone who weighs 65 kg, that's roughly 54 g a day. People who train regularly often aim higher — speak to a dietitian if you have specific goals.</p>
<p>Some people need more: regular gym-goers, older adults trying to preserve muscle, and anyone recovering from illness often benefit from a higher intake, while people with kidney conditions may need less — so it's worth getting personal advice.</p>
<p>Spread across three meals, that means each meal should carry a real portion of protein. Here's how to do it in a salad.</p>
<h2 id="7-protein-boosters-for-a-high-protein-vegetarian-salad">7 protein boosters for a high protein vegetarian salad</h2><h3 id="1-chana-chickpeas">1. Chana (chickpeas)</h3><p>Cooked chickpeas bring around 8–9 g of protein per 100 g plus plenty of fibre. They hold their shape well, which makes them perfect for salads. Ours go into the <a href="/menu/desi-mango-sprout-salad">Desi Mango &amp; Sprout Salad</a>.</p>
<h3 id="2-moong-and-other-sprouts">2. Moong and other sprouts</h3><p>Sprouted moong adds a fresh crunch and extra protein. Combining legumes like chana and moong also gives you a better mix of amino acids.</p>
<h3 id="3-paneer">3. Paneer</h3><p>Paneer is one of the richest vegetarian protein sources, at roughly 18 g per 100 g. Grill or toss it in a light marinade so it doesn't feel heavy.</p>
<h3 id="4-mozzarella-and-other-cheeses">4. Mozzarella and other cheeses</h3><p>Fresh mozzarella brings protein and creaminess. Our <a href="/menu/salad-caprese">Salad Caprese</a> pairs buffalo mozzarella with tomatoes, basil and pine nuts.</p>
<h3 id="5-hung-curd-and-greek-style-yoghurt">5. Hung curd and Greek-style yoghurt</h3><p>Strained yoghurt is higher in protein than regular curd and makes a brilliant creamy dressing base — try it with garlic, herbs and lemon.</p>
<h3 id="6-peanuts-nuts-and-seeds">6. Peanuts, nuts and seeds</h3><p>A spoonful of peanuts, walnuts, pumpkin seeds or sesame adds protein, healthy fats and crunch. Peanuts are especially good value; our <a href="/menu/asian-noodle-salad">Asian Noodle Salad</a> is finished with them.</p>
<h3 id="7-eggs-if-you-eat-them">7. Eggs (if you eat them)</h3><p>For eggetarians, two eggs add around 12 g of high-quality protein. Hard-boil a batch at the start of the week.</p>
<h2 id="putting-it-together-a-sample-bowl">Putting it together: a sample bowl</h2><ul>
<li>Base: lettuce, cucumber, tomato and shredded cabbage</li>
<li>Protein: 100 g chana + a handful of moong sprouts</li>
<li>Extra protein: 50 g grilled paneer</li>
<li>Crunch: 1 tablespoon roasted peanuts</li>
<li>Dressing: hung curd with lemon, mint and roasted cumin, on the side</li>
</ul>
<p>That one bowl comfortably delivers a meaningful share of your daily protein, with fibre and very little effort. Swap paneer for tofu if you prefer a lighter option, or add a boiled egg if you eat eggs.</p>
<h2 id="protein-in-common-salad-ingredients">Protein in common salad ingredients</h2><p>Approximate protein per 100 g (values vary by brand and preparation — check the label where you can):</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Ingredient</th>
<th>Protein (approx.)</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Peanuts, roasted</td>
<td>25 g</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mozzarella</td>
<td>20 g</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Paneer</td>
<td>18 g</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Eggs (about 2)</td>
<td>12 g</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Hung curd / Greek-style yoghurt</td>
<td>9–10 g</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Chickpeas (chana), cooked</td>
<td>8–9 g</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Moong, cooked</td>
<td>7 g</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The easiest way to build a high protein vegetarian salad is to combine two sources — for example chana with paneer, or sprouts with a yoghurt dressing and peanuts.</p>
<h2 id="common-mistakes-that-keep-protein-low">Common mistakes that keep protein low</h2><ul>
<li><strong>Treating protein as a garnish.</strong> A few cubes of paneer won't move the needle; aim for a palm-sized portion.</li>
<li><strong>Relying on one source.</strong> Mixing legumes, dairy and nuts gives you a better amino-acid profile and more variety.</li>
<li><strong>Skipping breakfast protein, then over-correcting at dinner.</strong> Spreading protein across the day is easier on digestion and helps you stay full.</li>
<li><strong>Choosing creamy dressings made mostly of oil.</strong> A yoghurt-based dressing adds protein instead of only fat.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="a-week-of-high-protein-vegetarian-salad-lunches">A week of high protein vegetarian salad lunches</h2><p>Need a plan? Here's a simple rotation that changes the protein every day:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Monday:</strong> chana and moong sprout salad with a curd-mint dressing.</li>
<li><strong>Tuesday:</strong> grilled paneer, cucumber, tomato and onion with lemon and chaat masala.</li>
<li><strong>Wednesday:</strong> mozzarella, tomato and basil over rocket with pesto and pine nuts.</li>
<li><strong>Thursday:</strong> rice noodles, shredded vegetables and peanuts in a sesame-soy dressing, topped with tofu or extra peanuts.</li>
<li><strong>Friday:</strong> roasted potatoes, apple and walnuts with boiled eggs or extra paneer.</li>
</ol>
<p>Prep chana, paneer and dressings on Sunday and Wednesday, and each lunch takes about five minutes to assemble.</p>
<h2 id="tips-for-making-it-a-habit">Tips for making it a habit</h2><ul>
<li><strong>Batch-cook your legumes.</strong> Cook chana once, cool it quickly and refrigerate; it's ready for salads for the next couple of days.</li>
<li><strong>Rotate your proteins.</strong> Different sources bring different nutrients and keep your lunches interesting.</li>
<li><strong>Don't forget the dressing.</strong> A protein-rich yoghurt dressing is an easy win.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you'd rather leave the cooking to us, our <a href="/plans/protein-pro">Protein Pro subscription</a> rotates our most protein-rich bowls on the days you choose.</p>
